# A hedge is a word or phrase that keeps the writer from committing to a
# claim (`this should fix the race`, `probably`, `to some extent`). A
# writer with real doubt can name the condition that the claim depends
# on, so the message offers that fix beside stating the claim. The rule
# hit 291 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 19 of 20 sampled hits
# softened a claim that the writer can state or tie to a condition.
# The miss sat in a quotation.
#
# Harper's Hedging rule matches three phrases, so this list carries the
# rest. `should work` and its kin count only after `it`, `this`, or
# `that`, where they hedge a claim about the code (`this should fix the
# race`). After `you` they give advice, which the technical-writing
# skill writes with `should`. `you should see` is the exception, because
# it hedges the result of a step (`run the build, and you should see the
# prompt`). It hit 12 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 12 of 12
# hedged a result. `should help` stays out, because the
# ability rule reports `help you`. `in a way` stays out too, because it
# also names a manner (`in a way that keeps the order`). The Optional
# rule of the same name holds `relatively`, `virtually`, `practically`,
# and `should` before a visible result. The opinion rule holds the
# phrases that frame a claim as someone's view. `kind of` and `sort of`
# count only before an adjective, adverb, or verb, because docs use them
# as nouns (`the kind of diff`). They also pass after a determiner (a
# word that introduces a noun, such as `the`, `a`, or `this`), as in `a
# sort of index`. `a bit` is left out, because a mask sets a bit. `a
# little bit` stays. `possibly` is left out too, because it gives the
# range of a value (`a non-nil, possibly empty map`). `rather` is left
# out as well, because `rather than` is a contrast and a `!than` guard
# misses it when a comment line breaks between the two words.
#
# `likely` skips `more likely` and `less likely`, which state a
# comparison (`cold caches are more likely to miss`), and `how likely`,
# which asks for a degree (`without saying how likely it is`). It also
# skips `likely to`, which names a probability (`pods likely to need
# more memory`), although that also passes the hedge `the fix is likely
# to resolve the race`. The expletive rule reports `it is likely that`,
# so `likely that` passes too. The filter reads the word before `likely`
# and narrows the finding back to it, so a sentence that opens with
# `Likely` passes. The word hit 43 lines in the measure.py corpus, and
# 42 of 43 hedged a claim. The miss was `the most likely pod eviction
# order`, where the probability is the point.
#
# `appears to` skips a determiner, pronoun, or proper noun after it,
# where it means `becomes visible to` (`the prompt appears to the
# user`).
#
# `fairly` and `quite` count only as degree words, before an
# adjective, an adverb, or (for `quite`) a determiner (`fairly
# standard`, `quite a large scope`). Before a verb, `fairly` means
# justly (`a normalizer that ignores whitespace can fairly ignore line
# breaks`).
expr main [<(![more, less, how] likely!(( )[to, that])), likely>, somewhat, arguably, perhaps, probably, maybe, (in essence), (!DET [kind, sort] of [ADJ, ADV, VERB]), essentially, basically, (a little bit), (to some extent), (to a certain extent), (more or less), (generally speaking), (for the most part), (in most cases), (it seems), (it seemed), (seems to), (seem to), ([appears, appear] to ![DET, PRON, PROPN]), (tends to), (tend to), presumably, (fairly [ADJ, ADV]), (quite [ADJ, ADV, DET]), apparently, seemingly, supposedly, ostensibly, (in theory), (in principle), (in a sense), ([it, this, that] should [work, fix, resolve, suffice]), ([it, this, that] should be [fine, enough, safe, ok, okay]), (you should see)]
let message "Hedge. State the claim, name the condition it depends on, or drop it."
let description "Flags words that soften a claim without adding information."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The printer takes the kind of each segment from the token."
allows "You should fix the typo before you merge."
allows "The cache evicts entries in a way that keeps the order."
allows "The mask sets a bit for each enabled flag."
allows "The walker visits the child rather than the alias."
allows "A sort of index maps each anchor to its node."
allows "What kind of error does it return?"
allows "Load returns a non-nil, possibly empty map."
allows "The prompt appears to the user after login."
allows "A normalizer that ignores whitespace can fairly ignore line breaks too."
allows "Cold caches are more likely to miss."
allows "The log does not say how likely a retry is."
allows "Pods likely to need more memory get a larger limit."
